interface.longDescription:
Ironwork is a strength-training log for iPhone and the web. Connect it to ChatGPT to ask about your own training and to draft programs you review in Ironwork.

What ChatGPT can read, after you allow it on Ironwork's consent screen:
- your finished workouts: exercises, sets, reps, weights, duration and volume;
- the history of one exercise: top sets, estimated 1RM and personal records with their dates;
- a summary over any range: sessions, volume, weekly working sets per muscle and body part, records set;
- how your main lifts are moving week by week, and where one has stalled;
- your programs, a program week by week, and how closely you are keeping to the one you follow;
- Ironwork's own help articles, with the app's exact labels.

What ChatGPT can add: drafts of programs (new, or a changed version of one you have), routines and custom exercises, marked with its name. Nothing changes until you open the draft in Ironwork and save it. ChatGPT can never change or delete what is there, and cannot log a workout for you. A draft program is checked against Ironwork's method (weekly sets per muscle for your goal, sets per exercise, days per muscle) before it is added.

The numbers are Ironwork's own: warm-ups are not counted as sets, a drop set is one set, volume is weight × reps, e1RM is Epley from sets of 1 to 12 reps.

What it never reads: Apple Health values, body weight, measurements, photos, check-ins, messages, friends, or anyone else's data, including a coach's other clients.

You need an Ironwork account (18 or older). Disconnect at any time in Ironwork › Profile › Privacy › Connected Apps; access ends at once.
